WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.026
"The Best Type of Sunscreen to Use"

00:00:10.202 --> 00:00:12.000
What kind of sunscreen
should you use?

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.460
Cream-based is preferable
to spray-on,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:15.810
since the completeness
of application

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.580
is more readily visualized.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.380
To help with adequate coverage,

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:20.760
spray-on sunscreens should be

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.220
rubbed on immediately
after spraying.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.260
Aerosolized sunscreens
are flammable

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.440
and can combust on skin
upon exposure to an open flame,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.020
even after the sunscreen
has dried.

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:33.300
What's more,
the safety of breathing in

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:34.680
aerosolized sunscreen chemicals

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.600
has not been
adequately studied,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:39.120
though frankly the same thing
could be said

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:40.860
about rubbing them
on your skin.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.740
The concerns
surrounding sunscreen safety

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:44.460
are three-fold:

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:47.280
increased
intentional sun exposure,

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:50.520
vitamin D deficiency,
and untoward effects

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.160
from systemic absorption
of sunscreen chemicals.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:58.260
In the 1800s, we first learned
that UVB caused sunburns

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:00.360
and formulated sunscreens
to block it.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.920
Nearly a century later,
we learned

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:03.540
about the contribution of UVA.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.400
Now, a full century later,

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:08.220
we are realizing that infrared
and visible light

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:09.840
may also be contributing
to cancer

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:11.100
and premature skin aging.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:13.920
Sunlight comes to us
in three major bands:

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:17.340
visible light, ultraviolet,
and infrared.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:19.800
Visible light includes
the colors of the rainbow—

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:23.040
red, orange, yellow, green,
blue, indigo, and violet,

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.240
or ROYGBIV.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.860
Ultraviolet—UV—is to the right
of violet in ROYGBIV,

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.900
and infrared
to the left of red.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.360
Sunscreen can protect us

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:35.280
against the free radical damage
of UV,

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.560
but half
of the free radical formation

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:38.640
in our skin from the sun

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.220
may be from the visible
or infrared spectrums,

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.740
which sunscreens
may not adequately cover.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:47.880
So, that's where sun avoidance
strategies come in,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:50.160
but you can imagine
how shade-seeking

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.200
could be undermined
by the solacement

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.360
of a sunscreen
security blanket.

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.100
What about vitamin D?

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.740
The theoretical concern
about sunscreen

00:01:58.740 --> 00:01:59.820
affecting vitamin D status

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.400
does not seem to manifest
in the real world,

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:04.860
likely because
the UV dose necessary

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:07.200
for vitamin D production
is so low,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.840
well before skin
turns a pinker shade.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.100
However, concerns
about the systemic absorption

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.380
of sunscreen chemicals
were underscored

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.480
by the recent FDA bombshell

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.780
that not a single one
of sunscreen chemicals

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:23.280
in current use
can be considered

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.200
generally recognized as safe.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:28.020
Only two active ingredients
got the green light,

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:31.140
the two non-chemical
"mineral" sunscreens:

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:33.120
titanium dioxide
and zinc oxide.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:36.480
The revelation was based
on a growing body of evidence

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:39.240
that transdermal, meaning
through-the-skin, absorption

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.380
of sunscreen chemicals

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:42.780
was greater than
we previously thought,

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:45.600
raising unevaluated
safety concerns.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:49.020
Unevaluated, because we
previously didn't think so much

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.400
got into our bloodstreams.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:52.920
In 2019, a study published

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:54.420
in the Journal of
the American Medical Association

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:56.580
found that under maximum
usage conditions

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.620
over a period of days,
the blood levels

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.480
of all the sunscreen chemicals
they tested

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.160
exceeded the FDA threshold

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:05.100
that could potentially waive
further safety testing.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:08.820
Then, in 2020, they found
that just a single application

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:11.100
of all the chemical sunscreens
they tested

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.720
surpassed the threshold.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.600
Now, just because they're
absorbed into our system,

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:17.700
doesn't necessarily mean
they're unsafe.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:20.460
It just means they need
to be tested for safety,

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:21.840
which, the FDA determined,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.360
the multibillion-dollar
sunscreen industry

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:26.100
has so far failed to do.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.220
The FDA concluded
that two ingredients

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.120
can be considered safe—

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.460
zinc oxide
and titanium dioxide,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.920
and two can be
considered unsafe—

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.800
P.A.B.A.
and trolamine salicylate.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.920
And the other 12 ingredients
that are currently marketed

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.740
have yet to be
sufficiently safety tested.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.500
According to a review of more
than 700 sunscreen brands,

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:48.300
it appears P.A.B.A.
and trolamine

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.640
are no longer being marketed
in sunscreens

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:51.480
sold in the United States,

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:54.180
but they still may be available
in other countries.

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:56.460
Until the data are in
on the rest

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.600
of the chemical sunscreens,

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.780
I would recommend sticking
to the two mineral sunscreens.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:04.080
Historically, they've tended
to be thicker and whiter,

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.780
which could lead
to even more severe underdosing,

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:08.700
but newer
micronized formulations

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.740
with smaller
mineral particle sizes

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:12.420
tend to be less noticeable.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:15.660
This is particularly an issue
for those with darker skin.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.540
The average built-in SPF
of black skin

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:21.960
(also known in the medical
literature as "ethnic skin"

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:27.360
or "SOC," skin of color)
is about 13 naturally,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.640
compared with only about three
for white skin.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.220
Though there haven't been
any interventional studies

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.740
on sunscreen effectiveness
for skin cancer prevention

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.360
in people with dark skin,

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:40.440
SPF 13 is not considered
sufficient sun protection;

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.080
so, the American Academy
of Dermatology recommends

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.900
regular sunscreen use
with an SPF of 30 or higher

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.940
for people of all skin types.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.280
Unfortunately,
only about 12 percent

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.040
of non-Hispanic Blacks
and 31 percent of Hispanics

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.960
report regularly using
sunscreen,

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:57.900
compared to about 44 percent
of non-Hispanic whites.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.600
Despite this,
the incidence of melanoma,

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.880
the deadliest skin cancer,
is five times lower

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.500
in Hispanics compared to whites

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.840
and more than 25 times lower
among Blacks.

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:10.260
However, the mortality rate
if you do get it

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:11.940
is higher among
African Americans,

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.820
presumed to be due
from delayed diagnosis.

